      "id": "171a98ae-1f31-44e9-bace-0e63d701f06d",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Great, this example is where **lambdas + functional interfaces + generics** all come together.\nI’ll explain it in **layers**, so you clearly see *what problem this code solves* and *why it’s powerful*.\n\n---\n\n# 🧩 Big Picture First\n\nThis code shows:\n\n> **“How to pass behavior (logic) as a parameter using lambdas, in a type-safe way.”**\n\nYou’re building a **generic calculator** that can:\n\n* add numbers\n* divide numbers\n* combine strings\n  👉 **without changing the calculator method**\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ The `Operation<T>` functional interface\n\n```java\n@FunctionalInterface\npublic interface Operation<T> {\n    T operate(T value1, T value2);\n}\n```\n\n### What this means\n\n* `@FunctionalInterface` → must have **exactly ONE abstract method**\n* Generic type `T` → works with **any data type**\n* Method contract:\n\n  ```java\n  T operate(T value1, T value2);\n  ```\n\n### Mental model\n\nThink of this as saying:\n\n> “Give me **any operation** that takes two `T`s and returns one `T`.”\n\nThis is what allows lambdas later.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ The generic `calculator` method\n\n```java\npublic static <T> T calculator(Operation<T> function, T value1, T value2) {\n\n    T result = function.operate(value1, value2);\n    System.out.println(\"Result of operation: \" + result);\n    return result;\n}\n```\n\n### Break it down\n\n#### `<T>`\n\n* Method-level generic\n* `T` is decided **at the call site**\n\n#### Parameters\n\n```java\nOperation<T> function  // behavior\nT value1               // data\nT value2               // data\n```\n\nThis is **strategy pattern**, simplified with lambdas.\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ First lambda: Integer addition\n\n```java\nint result = calculator((var a, var b) -> a + b, 5, 2);\n```\n\n### What Java infers\n\n* `value1 = 5`, `value2 = 2` → `T = Integer`\n* So:\n\n  ```java\n  Operation<Integer>\n  ```\n\n### Lambda mapping\n\n```java\n(var a, var b) -> a + b\n```\n\nis treated as:\n\n```java\nInteger operate(Integer a, Integer b) {\n    return a + b;\n}\n```\n\n### Output\n\n```\nResult of operation: 7\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ Second lambda: Double division\n\n```java\nvar result2 = calculator((a, b) -> a / b, 10.0, 2.5);\n```\n\n### Type inference here\n\n* `10.0`, `2.5` → `Double`\n* Lambda becomes:\n\n  ```java\n  Double operate(Double a, Double b)\n  ```\n\n### Output\n\n```\nResult of operation: 4.0\n```\n\n💡 **Same method, different behavior, different type**\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Third lambda: String operation\n\n```java\nvar result3 = calculator(\n        (a, b) -> a.toUpperCase() + \" \" + b.toUpperCase(),\n        \"Ralph\", \"Kramden\");\n```\n\n### What happens now\n\n* `T = String`\n* Lambda logic:\n\n  ```java\n  String operate(String a, String b) {\n      return a.toUpperCase() + \" \" + b.toUpperCase();\n  }\n  ```\n\n### Output\n\n```\nResult of operation: RALPH KRAMDEN\n```\n\n🚀 Same calculator, **completely different meaning**\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Why lambdas work here (important)\n\nBecause `Operation<T>`:\n\n* has **one abstract method**\n* matches lambda shape:\n\n  ```\n  (T, T) -> T\n  ```\n\nIf you added **another abstract method**, this would **fail**.\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Why `var` works in lambdas\n\n```java\n(var a, var b) -> a + b\n```\n\nJava infers:\n\n* type from `Operation<T>`\n* which is inferred from method arguments\n\nRules reminder:\n\n* If you use `var`, **all parameters must use `var`**\n* You can also write:\n\n  ```java\n  (a, b) -> a + b\n  ```\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ What this code is REALLY teaching you\n\n### ✔ Functional programming\n\nPassing logic as data\n\n### ✔ Generics\n\nOne method works for **any type**\n\n### ✔ Type inference\n\nJava figures out everything at compile time\n\n### ✔ Strategy pattern (modern Java)\n\nDifferent algorithms, same interface\n\n---\n\n## 9️⃣ Equivalent old-school Java (for understanding)\n\nWhat this replaces:\n\n```java\ncalculator(new Operation<Integer>() {\n    @Override\n    public Integer operate(Integer a, Integer b) {\n        return a + b;\n    }\n}, 5, 2);\n```\n\nLambdas remove **boilerplate**, not logic.\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Final mental model (keep this)\n\n> **Lambda = implementation of a functional interface method, passed inline**\n\n> **Generic + lambda = reusable behavior**\n\n---\n\n## 🔜 What you’re now ready for\n\nNext topics will feel *natural* now:\n\n* Method references (`String::toUpperCase`)\n* Built-in functional interfaces (`Function`, `BiFunction`)\n* Streams (`map`, `filter`, `reduce`)\n\nIf you want, next I can:\n\n* rewrite this using **built-in functional interfaces**\n* explain **why lambdas can’t modify local variables**\n* show **method reference versions**\n\nJust tell me 👍\n",
